Now, since {EA} is incommensurable in length with {AB}, therefore {EA} and {AB} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only, therefore {AK}, that is, the sum of the squares on {MN} and {NO}, is medial. [!just X.21]

Again, since {ED} is incommensurable in length with {AB}, therefore {FE} is also incommensurable with {EK}.  Therefore {FE} and {EK} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only.  Therefore {EL}, that is, {MR}, that is, the rectangle {MN} by {NO}, is medial. [!just X.13; X.21]

Since {AE} is incommensurable with {EF}, therefore {AK} is also incommensurable with {EL}. [!just VI.1, X.11]

But {AK} is the sum of the squares on {MN} and {NO}, and {EL} is the rectangle {MN} by {NO}, therefore the sum of the squares on {MN} and {NO} is incommensurable with the rectangle {MN} by {NO}. And each of them is medial, and {MN} and {NO} are incommensurable in square.

Therefore {MO} is the side of the sum of two medial areas, and is the side of {AC}. [!just X.41]

Therefore, *if an area is contained by a rational straight line and the sixth binomial, then the side of the area is the irrational straight line called the side of the sum of two medial areas.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
